# Problem records

What the website shows on a problem's page: the problem as its source gave it,
its OPDP profile, where it stands in the factory, and its formal statements.
Fields a source does not provide are the empty text (a page cannot hold an
optional field, LOGOS_ISSUES.md 11), so the page shows them as absent rather
than as zero.

## Note
One problem. `stage` and `rankBp` (its rank in basis points) are its node's; the block after them is the
problem as ingested; the OPDP block is the atlas profile (`opdp_profile.lg`), empty for a
problem the atlas does not cover. `attempts` counts every recorded attempt,
`claimed` the ones whose worker reported a proof, and `attested` the verdicts the
trusted verifier signed. `trailCheck` is what re-hashing the trail found:
`intact`, `empty`, or the first broken link.

## A ProblemView has:
    an id, which is Text.
    a title, which is Text.
    a status, which is Text.
    a stage, which is Text.
    a rankBp, which is Int.
    a source, which is Text.
    a cohort, which is Text.
    a collection, which is Text.
    a category, which is Text.
    a statementInformal, which is Text.
    a leanStatement, which is Text.
    a leanModule, which is Text.
    a leanDeclaration, which is Text.
    an externalKey, which is Text.
    a crossRef, which is Text.
    a difficultyLabel, which is Text.
    a difficulty, which is Text.
    a tier, which is Text.
    an aiFit, which is Text.
    an aiDifficulty, which is Text.
    a tractability, which is Text.
    a progress, which is Text.
    a formalization, which is Text.
    a verification, which is Text.
    a toolLeverage, which is Text.
    a route, which is Text.
    an effortHours, which is Text.
    an explanation, which is Text.
    an attempts, which is Int.
    a claimed, which is Int.
    an attested, which is Int.
    a spentMicros, which is Int.
    a trailLength, which is Int.
    a deadEnds, which is Int.
    an openUnits, which is Int.
    a trailCheck, which is Text.

## Note
One formal statement of a problem: its LOGOS kernel document, the goal a proof
must prove, the hash a proof is bound to, how it was made (route A from Lean, B
from an informal problem through Lean, C directly) and how its review stands.

## A StatementRow has:
    an id, which is Text.
    a goalName, which is Text.
    a sha256, which is Text.
    a reviewState, which is Text.
    a route, which is Text.
    an author, which is Text.
    a model, which is Text.
    a createdAt, which is Int.
    a document, which is Text.

## Note
One node below a problem: a statement, or a lemma a decomposition left open or
proved. `parentId` is the node it hangs from, so the rows draw as a tree.

## A LemmaRow has:
    an id, which is Text.
    a parentId, which is Text.
    a kind, which is Text.
    a label, which is Text.
    a stage, which is Text.
    a depth, which is Int.

## Note
One verdict of the trusted verifier: what it judged, against which statement and
document hashes, with which pinned toolchain, signed by which key, and when.

## A ResultRow has:
    an id, which is Text.
    a subjectKind, which is Text.
    a subjectId, which is Text.
    a verdictKind, which is Text.
    a verifier, which is Text.
    a statementSha, which is Text.
    a documentSha, which is Text.
    a toolchainSha, which is Text.
    a publicKeySha, which is Text.
    a signature, which is Text.
    a createdAt, which is Int.
